    "text_blocks": [
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "ACE inhibitors and ARBs (Angiotensin Receptor Blockers) are both effective first-line treatments for high blood pressure, but ARBs are generally better tolerated with fewer side effects.",
        "snippet_links": [
          {
            "text": "ARBs (Angiotensin Receptor Blockers)",
            "link": "https://gpnotebook.com/en-IE/pages/cardiovascular-medicine/angiotensin-receptor-blockers-arb-in-hypertension"
          }
        ],
        "snippet_highlighted_words": [
          "ARBs are generally better tolerated with fewer side effects"
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "Both drug classes lower blood pressure by blocking the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system (RAAS), which relaxes blood vessels and protects the heart and kidneys. However, they have distinct differences in side effects and clinical use."
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "ACE Inhibitors"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "How they work: Block the enzyme that produces angiotensin II, lowering blood pressure and reducing stress on the heart. Common names end in (e.g., lisinopril, enalapril)."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Side effects: Often cause a persistent, dry, nagging cough in 5% to 20% of patients. Carry a small risk of angioedema (dangerous swelling of the lips, tongue, or throat)."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Pros: Extensive long-term clinical data showing strong cardiovascular benefits, particularly in patients with heart failure. Often lower cost."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "ARBs (Angiotensin Receptor Blockers)"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "How they work: Block the receptors that angiotensin II binds to, achieving a similar relaxation of blood vessels without affecting the chemical pathway that triggers a cough. Common names end in (e.g., losartan, valsartan)."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Side effects: Rarely cause a dry cough and have a much lower risk of angioedema."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Pros: Highly effective blood pressure control with much better patient adherence because they are better tolerated."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Shared Side Effects and Warnings"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "High potassium: Both classes can raise blood potassium levels (hyperkalemia ) and require monitoring."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Kidney function: Both can cause a mild, temporary rise in serum creatinine when starting treatment, but they protect long-term kidney health in chronic kidney disease and diabetes."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Pregnancy warning: Never use either an ACE inhibitor or an ARB during pregnancy as they cause severe fetal harm."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Never combine: Do not take an ACE inhibitor and an ARB together; the combination increases risks of kidney failure and high potassium without added benefit."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "If you'd like, share:"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "If you or your patient are experiencing a side effect like a cough"
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Any underlying health conditions like diabetes or kidney disease"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "I can help explain how these factors influence medication choice."
      }
    ],
